\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 With parliamentary elections in Austria barely more than a month away, it seems as if no one can stop the march of the opposition Freedom Party of Austria (FP), which is campaigning on a hard anti-immigration platform.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 The latest polls suggest that the party has a 10 percentage point lead over its two main rivals, the governing centre-right Austrian People's Party (VP) and the opposition Social Democrats (SP). According to a survey by INSA, FP is projected to win 31% of the votes on September 29th, SP 21%, and VP-which has governed the country since 2017, first in a coalition with FP, and then the Greens-19%.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 This means that, for the first time, neither of the two mainstream parties-VP and SP-who have taken turns ruling the country since the end of World War II, is projected to win. If the forecasts are correct, FP will gain its largest share of the votes since the election in 1999, when it received 27%.}
